simulacrum-server is the gate in front of merc-mud (the "Simulacrum" demo at simulacrum.shatteredarchive.dev) — the ONLY thing standing between the public internet and the raw engine, since merc-mud's own port is never published outside the docker network it shares this service with. Two independent listeners in one process: a small Express surface (port 65000 — health, the SSO sign-in hand-off, and the one auth-gated route, POST /api/access-code) and a plain TCP relay (port 65001 — the actual play connection). Its central invariant is the connection-time gate: the relay prompts for an access code before it ever dials merc-mud, and an anonymous or under-tier connection attempt never reaches the engine at all — this is deliberately NOT a client-side or game-server-side check, because the standalone mobile app dials merc-mud directly over raw TCP with no gate of its own to hook into. Access codes are minted only for trusted-tier-or-above accounts (checked live against a role store SHARED with mud-builder-server via a single-file bind mount onto its roles.json — a grant or revocation in one service's Roles tab takes effect on the other's very next connection attempt, not next mint). Once admitted, the relay sends a `#SIMACCT <accountId> <username>\n` preamble to merc-mud as the literal first bytes of the session, before anything the human types — that is how a brand-new in-game character gets linked back to its hub account (see the follow-on plan, 20260816-0724) without a second code or any change to merc-mud's own login flow beyond intercepting that one preamble line.
